Question to be addressed:

Orchids are one of the most diverse plant families, containing roughly 700 genera and thousands of species with unique physical characteristics. We want to investigate the efficacy of chloroplast trnH-psbA DNA barcode to distinguish between species within the Orchidaceae family. We also want to examine how genetically similar the sampled orchids are and whether their DNA barcode sequences reflect their taxonomic classifications. 

-	How does genetic variation in the chloroplast trnH-psbA barcode differ among orchid species and genera within the UWEC greenhouse collection? 
 
How to address the question
We propose collecting leaf samples from approximately 6 different orchid species within the UWEC greenhouse. We will isolate DNA from the collected samples and use PCR to amplify the chloroplast trnH-psbA barcode region. The amplified DNA fragments will be sent for sequencing. Once we obtain the sequences, we will use bioinformatics tools to compare the barcode sequences against publicly available sequences in databases such as BOLD systems and NCBI GenBank. We will then compare the genetic similarities between the sampled orchids and determine whether the trnH-psbA barcode can distinguish different orchid species and potentially reveal relationships between different orchid genera. 

